      'When you feel confused about your recovery — "Am I doing this right? Why is this so hard?" — that confusion is actually healthy. It means you\'re questioning, investigating, not just blindly following. When you feel pain — grief, shame, loneliness, fear — that pain is information. It\'s cracking you open so that compassion can get in.',
      "The problem isn't the confusion or the pain. The problem is fighting against them, trying to make them go away, believing they shouldn't be there. What brain science shows us is this: emotional avoidance actually increases suffering. When you suppress or fight painful emotions, the suppression itself creates additional distress. But when you allow emotions with curiosity rather than judgment, their intensity diminishes and their information becomes available.",
